{
  "dom_id": "step-q208786",
  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "Express the equation of the line that passes through the points $(-3,-2)$ and $(-1,4)$ in standard form.\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=$\n{{field-2}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, we will construct the point-slope form of the line. Then, we will simplify it into standard form.\n\nTo construct the point-slope form of the line, we need a point and a slope. We already have two points, so we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{4-(-2)}{-1-(-3)}=\\frac{6}{2}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use this slope along with one of the points to write the point-slope form equation of the line. It doesn't matter which point we choose, but let's choose $(-1,4).$ The point-slope equation is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =3(x-(-1)) \\\\ y-4 & =3(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify the point-slope formula into standard form, moving the variables to the left side and the constant to the right side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-4 & =3(x+1) \\\\ y-4 & =3x+3 \\\\ y & =3x+7 \\\\ -3x+y & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply by $-1$ to make the $x$-coefficient positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x+y & =7 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-3x+y) & =(-1)\\cdot 7 \\\\ 3x-y & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the standard form of the equation is \n\n$$\n3x-y=-7.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
